 Site 157 carries an exceptionally faint forward-reading impression; it is retained with extensive local doubts rather than treated as blank. The clearer opening on site 158 is headed ``Formes lisses'', with ``augmentations et singularités élémentaires'' inserted and the earlier continuation ``formes quadratiques lisses'' deleted. Its opening note explicitly revisits the preceding manuscript, now called SUP. It recalls the correction of ``non dégénérée'' to ``dégénérée'' in definition 1 and discusses the characteristic-two example $x^2+yz=0$. The author uses that example to explain his proposed vocabulary for smooth quadratic forms, nondegenerate quadratic forms, ordinary or elementary quadratic singularities, and supersingularity. The text states its relation between smoothness and nondegeneracy, including the rank and residual-characteristic qualifications, and asks the recipient's opinion about replacing ``ordinaire'' by ``élémentaire'' and extending the term to arbitrary multiplicity. Site 159 says that proposition 7 of SUP is intended to retain supersingularity in its statement, proposes a further closed subscheme of ultra-singular zeros using the ``discriminant corrigé'', and expressly requires the terminology on SUP page 10 to be revised as well. It also identifies IV 23.9.2 and the conditions of 8.8 as needing a change. The ensuing claim about the inseparability degree on site 160 is qualified by the author's own ``je crois'' and ``sans garantie''; the guide retains that status and supplies no missing verification. The overwritten letters, local cancellations and inserted terms remain visible in the transcription. See \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=158}{site 157}, \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=159}{site 158}, \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=160}{site 159} and \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=161}{site 160}.
 
 \paragraph{IV 17.15.1--15.2, the definitions and their written dependencies (sites 160--162).}
-Number 15.1 starts from a finite locally free Module $E$ on $S$ and a section $\phi$ of $\mathrm{Sym}^n(E)$, identifies it with a section of $\mathcal O_{P(E)}(n)$ by an explicit reference to Chapter III, and defines a smooth form through smoothness of $V(\phi)$ together with the stated nonvanishing condition on every fiber. It discusses fiberwise testing and change of the ground field, then gives the polynomial criterion in terms of $\phi$ and its partial derivatives, expressly invoking the Jacobian criterion. The inequality on $n$ is overwritten; the small inserted condition near the criterion is still doubtful, and the margin separately observes that degree zero gives the invertible sections of $\mathcal O_S$. The written rank exponent of $E$ and the variable list $X_0,\ldots,X_r$ use different letters and have been retained. Number 15.2 defines an elementary augmentation of multiplicity $n$ by three stated requirements: local freeness of $J/J^2$, generation of the graded kernel by an invertible locally direct summand in degree $n$, and smoothness of its local generating form. Its displayed map is the symmetric-algebra comparison with $\mathrm{gr}_J(\mathcal O_X)$; $K$ is added as the name of the degree-$n$ kernel, and $\mu$ is handwritten over an obscured earlier letter. The accompanying note proposes formulating the last two requirements through a smooth invertible submodule, referring back to 15.1. The text then defines the corresponding language for augmented rings, local rings, points of a prescheme and geometric singularities over a field, and asks for a remark connecting the multiplicity with Samuel's notion. Numbers 15.3 and 15.4 request the multiplicity-$n$ variants of SUP proposition 8, proposition 9 and corollary 10; they provide instructions for further writing rather than new proofs here. Number 15.5 similarly requests the definition for a section of the structure sheaf or a locally free Module. See \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=161}{site 160}, \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=162}{site 161} and \href{https://grothendieck.umontpellier.fr/web/viewer.html?file=../25.pdf#page=163}{site 162}.
